    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Okay, thanks for the clarification on the breach part. That makes more sense. \n\nOne more thing though—the clause also says I have to \"defend\" them. I understand \"indemnify\" means paying them back for losses, but what does \"defend\" actually imply for me? Does that mean I'm responsible for their legal fees and the cost of lawyers if someone sues them because of something I did? I want to be sure I understand the full scope of the financial risk.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"defend\" clause is a bit more nuanced, but it's generally interpreted to mean that the Recipient will take reasonable steps to defend the Disclosing Party against any claims or lawsuits that may arise from the Recipient's actions.\n\nIn the context of the indemnification clause, \"defend\" typically means that the Recipient will:\n\n* Take reasonable steps to investigate and address any claims or allegations made against the Disclosing Party\n* Cooperate with the Disclosing Party in defending against any claims or lawsuits\n* Not admit liability or make any statements that could be seen as admitting fault\n\nHowever, it does not necessarily mean that the Recipient will bear the full cost of defending the Disclosing Party, including lawyers' fees. The Recipient's obligations under the \"defend\" clause are typically limited to taking reasonable steps to defend the Disclosing Party, and the Disclosing Party is usually responsible for paying their own lawyers' fees and costs.\n\nThink of it like this: imagine you're a freelancer who is hired to work on a project, and someone sues the client for a mistake that you didn't make. Your role is to defend the client, but you're not responsible for paying the client's lawyers' fees. You",
